## Example

Let $c_{00} \subset l^1$ be the subspace of all sequences with only finitely many nonzero terms:

$$c_{00} = \bigl\{ x = (x_n)_{n\geq 1} \in l^1 : x_n = 0 \text{ for all but finitely many } n \bigr\}.$$

I claim $c_{00}$ is a non-closed subspace of the Banach space $l^1$.

## Proof

**$c_{00}$ is a subspace.** It contains $0$. If $x, y \in c_{00}$ and $\alpha, \beta \in \mathbb{R}$ (or $\mathbb{C}$), then $\alpha x + \beta y$ is still nonzero only on the (finite) union of the supports of $x$ and $y$, hence $\alpha x + \beta y \in c_{00}$. Also $c_{00} \subseteq l^1$ since a finitely supported sequence is trivially absolutely summable.

**$c_{00}$ is not closed.** For each $k \geq 1$ define

$$x^{(k)} = \Bigl(\tfrac12,\, \tfrac14,\, \tfrac18,\, \dots,\, \tfrac{1}{2^k},\, 0,\, 0,\, \dots\Bigr), \qquad x^{(k)}_n = \begin{cases} 2^{-n}, & 1 \leq n \leq k, \\ 0, & n > k. \end{cases}$$

Each $x^{(k)}$ has finite support, so $x^{(k)} \in c_{00} \subseteq l^1$.

Let $x = (2^{-1}, 2^{-2}, 2^{-3}, \dots)$, i.e. $x_n = 2^{-n}$ for all $n \geq 1$. Then

$$\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} |x_n| = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} 2^{-n} = 1 < \infty,$$

so $x \in l^1$. But $x_n \neq 0$ for every $n$, so $x \notin c_{00}$.

Now compute the $l^1$-distance:

$$\bigl\| x^{(k)} - x \bigr\|_1 = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \bigl| x^{(k)}_n - x_n \bigr| = \sum_{n=k+1}^{\infty} 2^{-n} = 2^{-k} \xrightarrow{k \to \infty} 0.$$

Thus $x^{(k)} \to x$ in the $l^1$-norm. We have produced a sequence $(x^{(k)})$ in $c_{00}$ converging (in $l^1$) to a point $x \in l^1 \setminus c_{00}$.

Therefore $c_{00}$ is a subspace of $l^1$ that is **not closed**: it fails to contain all its $l^1$-limit points. $\blacksquare$

